    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> and governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>, leaving the emperor and court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> as figureheads.</li><li>From 1203 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a> held real power as shikken, regents of the shōgun, while Fujiwara and imperial princes served as figurehead shōguns.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> held off the Mongol invasions of 1274 and 1281, but their cost and the lack of rewards for its warriors weakened it before its fall in 1333.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitta-yoshisada">Nitta Yoshisada</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> in 1333, about 870 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, including the last three regents, committed suicide at their family temple, Tōshō-ji.</p></aside>
    </section>

