    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In earlier Japanese history civilian power lay with the emperor and his regents, drawn from the Imperial Court and the aristocratic clans, and military affairs were handled under the civil government. From 1180 to 1185 the Taira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-clan">Minamoto clans</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> defeated the Taira and in victory seized power from the civil aristocracy, reducing the emperor and his court to symbolic figureheads.</p><p>Historians disagree about the year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> began. The older and once most popular view took 1192, when Yoritomo was appointed Seii Taishōgun; the later prevailing view took 1185, when he established the shugo, who controlled military and police power in the regions, and the jitō, in charge of tax collection and land administration. Japanese history textbooks of 2016 give no single year. Yoritomo governed autocratically from the eastern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>, while the emperor and court stayed in the official capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> rested on the land. Its stewards, the jitō, were placed in the manors (shōen) and received revenues from them in return for military service. They worked alongside the gesu, who delivered the manor's dues to its proprietor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, so that the dual system of military and civil government reached down to the manor itself.</p><p>Warriors expected land as a reward for service. The defensive war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> left no gains to distribute to those who had fought, which led to discontent. For two decades after 1281 the shogunate kept watch against another invasion, and the strain on the military, the financial expenditure and the cost of building defensive walls weakened the regime considerably. The Hōjō monopoly of power, together with these unrewarded sacrifices, fed resentment among its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s greatest test came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>. Fifty years earlier it had agreed to Korean demands to deal with the wokou raiders, and the resulting good relations meant the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a>, under Mongol occupation, passed intelligence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The shogunate rejected Kublai's demands for submission. In 1274 a combined force of about 40,000 Mongols and Korean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> landed with some success, but the defenders were not routed, and a typhoon destroyed one-third of the invading force.</p><p>When Kublai again demanded submission, the Hōjō had the messengers executed and prepared: a wall was built round the hinterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakata">Hakata</a> Bay, defensive posts and garrison lists were set up and ships were built to harass the invaders. In 1281 some 50,000 Mongol, Korean and Chinese troops and about 100,000 conscripts from the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> fought for some seven weeks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> without strategic gain. Another typhoon destroyed at least one-third of the Mongol force, and troops left ashore were slaughtered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. The storm was credited as the "divine wind", <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> worked within the framework of the Heian system of Imperial rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo</a> made a chancellery, the mandokoro, his principal organ of government; under the Hōjō a separate council, the hyōjōshū, became its focus. Military governors (shugo) were appointed over the provinces, mostly from powerful local families, alongside the civil governors (kokushi) sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. In 1232 the government issued the legal code Goseibai Shikimoku, used into the Muromachi period, and set up a court of appeals, the Moncho-jo.</p><p>After Yoritomo's death in 1199, Hōjō Tokimasa claimed the regency (shikken), which became hereditary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, and his daughter Hōjō Masako became so powerful that she was called the "nun shogun". The Minamoto line ended when Sanetomo was assassinated in 1219. Later shōguns came from the Fujiwara and then from imperial princes, while the Hōjō regents ruled. The regime lasted 135 years under 9 shōguns and 16 regents.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> began the long age of military government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. By taking power from the civil aristocracy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo</a> made the emperor and court figureheads, a pattern the later shogunates kept. Its offices, the shugo over the provinces and the jitō in the manors, carried military rule into every province and down to the manor, and its legal code, the Goseibai Shikimoku, remained in use into the following period.</p><p>Its system was a pyramid of nominal and real authority: the Hōjō regents had taken power from the Minamoto, who had taken it from the emperor, yet regents, shōguns and emperors all kept their titles side by side. Its defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, in which the invasions were ended by heavy losses and the exhaustion of the Korean state that supplied them, gave rise to the idea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a>, the "divine wind" that saved Japan from foreign invasion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The power of the Hōjō went unchallenged after the Jōkyū War until 1324, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> plotted to overthrow them; the plot was discovered almost at once. In 1331 Go-Daigo took up arms against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> and exiled to Oki Island. When a warlord came to the exiled emperor's aid, the Hōjō sent Takauji to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Once there, though, he changed sides and supported Go-Daigo.</p><p>At the same time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitta-yoshisada">Nitta Yoshisada</a>, another warlord loyal to the emperor, attacked and took Kamakura in 1333. About 870 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, among them the last three regents, committed suicide at their family temple, Tōshō-ji, whose ruins have been found in today's Ōmachi. Imperial rule was restored in the Kenmu Restoration until 1336, when Ashikaga Takauji made himself shōgun and founded the Ashikaga shogunate.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from Kamakura between 1185 and 1333. Its real masters from 1203 were the Hōjō regents. It built a system of shugo and jitō, issued the Goseibai Shikimoku and repelled two Mongol invasions, but their cost weakened it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a>'s supporters destroyed it.</em></p>

